{"records":[{"id":"7bd5bhmhmz","title":"Genome-scale identification of SARS-CoV-2 and pan-coronavirus host factor networks. Schneider et al.","description":"The COVID-19 pandemic has claimed the lives of more than one million people worldwide. The causative agent, SARS-CoV-2, is a member of the Coronaviridae family, which are viruses that cause respiratory infections of varying severity. The cellular host factors and pathways co-opted by SARS- CoV-2 and other coronaviruses in the execution of their life cycles remain ill-defined. To develop an extensive compendium of host factors required for infection by SARS-CoV-2 and three seasonal coronaviruses (HCoV-OC43, HCoV-NL63, and HCoV-229E), we performed parallel genome-scale CRISPR knockout screens. These screens uncovered multiple host factors and pathways with pan- coronavirus and virus-specific functional roles, including major dependency on glycosaminoglycan biosynthesis, SREBP signaling, BMP signaling, and glycosylphosphatidylinositol biosynthesis, as well as a requirement for several poorly characterized proteins. We identified an absolute requirement for the VTT-domain containing protein TMEM41B for infection by SARS-CoV-2 and three seasonal coronaviruses. This human Coronaviridae host factor compendium represents a rich resource to develop new therapeutic strategies for acute COVID-19 and potential future coronavirus spillover events.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-03T09:15:53.589Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7bd5bhmhmz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7bd5bhmhmz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"JT","last_name":"Poirier","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2hght7dmk5","title":"Deubiquitinases in macrophages infected with Salmonella enterica or Yersinia enterocolitica","description":"We used an activity-based proteomics screen of deubiquitinases in human macrophages to identify several deubiquitinases regulated in THP-1 derived macrophages during infection with Salmonella Typhimurium and Yersinia enterocolitica at 2 and 18 hours of infection.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-03-03T14:49:31.81Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2hght7dmk5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2hght7dmk5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kamil","last_name":"Hercik","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mariola","last_name":"Edelmann","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"54v7hn8hvz","title":"Legionella pneumophila exploits the endo-lysosomal network for phagosome biogenesis","description":"L. pneumophila strains harboring wild-type rpsL such as Lp02rpsLWT cannot replicate in mouse bone marrow-derived macrophages (BMDMs) due to induction of extensive lysosome damage and apoptosis. The mechanism of this unique infection-induced cell death remains unknown. Using a genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 screening, we identified Hmg20a and Nol9 as host factors important for restricting strain Lp02rpsLWT in BMDMs. Depletion of Hmg20a protects macrophages from infection-induced lysosomal damage and apoptosis, allowing productive bacterial replication. The restriction imposed by Hmg20a was mediated by repressing the expression of several endo-lysosomal proteins, including the small GTPase Rab7. We found that SUMOylated Rab7 is recruited to the bacterial phagosome via SulF, a Dot/Icm effector that harbors a SUMO-interacting motif (SIM). Moreover, overexpression of Rab7 rescues intracellular growth of strain Lp02rpsLWT in BMDMs. Our results establish that L. pneumophila exploits the lysosomal network for the biogenesis of its phagosome in BMDMs.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-09-07T16:49:43.441Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/54v7hn8hvz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/54v7hn8hvz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Chuang","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nxvdzybwhf","title":"Illuminating host-mycobacterial interactions with genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screens","description":"The data deposited here contains genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screen data, including the positive hits, the negative hits, and non-targeting controls. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-30T11:32:01.108Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nxvdzybwhf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nxvdzybwhf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"Lai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","High-Throughput Sequencing","Tuberculosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gcdpsxd6d5","title":"RNAi screening using HK-2 cells: insights into the roles of soluble ACE2 in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Yeung et al","description":"Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) can cause acute respiratory disease and multiorgan failure. Finding human host factors that are essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection could facilitate the formulation of treatment strategies. Using a human kidney cell line—HK-2—that is highly susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, we performed a genome-wide RNAi screening and identified virus dependency factors (VDFs), which play regulatory roles in biological pathways linked to clinical manifestations of SARS-CoV-2 infection. Importantly, we discovered an overlooked role of a secretory form of SARS-CoV-2 receptor, soluble angiotensin converting enzyme 2 (sACE2), in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Further investigations revealed that SARS-CoV-2 exploits receptor-mediated endocytosis through interaction between its spike with sACE2 or sACE2-vasopressin via AT1 or AVPR1B, respectively. Our identification of new VDFs and the regulatory effect of sACE2 on SARS-CoV-2 infection shed new insights into pathogenesis and cell entry mechanism of SARS-CoV-2 as well as potential treatment strategies for COVID-19.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-22T23:41:07.557Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gcdpsxd6d5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gcdpsxd6d5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Man Lung","last_name":"Yeung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","RNA Knockdown"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"72tr8jj8y4","title":"RNAi screening using HK-2 cells: insights into the roles of soluble ACE2 in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Yeung et al","description":"Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) can cause acute respiratory disease and multiorgan failure. Finding human host factors that are essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection could facilitate the formulation of treatment strategies. Using a human kidney cell line—HK-2—that is highly susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, we performed a genome-wide RNAi screening and identified virus dependency factors (VDFs), which play regulatory roles in biological pathways linked to clinical manifestations of SARS-CoV-2 infection. Importantly, we discovered an overlooked role of a secretory form of SARS-CoV-2 receptor, soluble angiotensin converting enzyme 2 (sACE2), in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Further investigations revealed that SARS-CoV-2 exploits receptor-mediated endocytosis through interaction between its spike with sACE2 or sACE2-vasopressin via AT1 or AVPR1B, respectively. Our identification of new VDFs and the regulatory effect of sACE2 on SARS-CoV-2 infection shed new insights into pathogenesis and cell entry mechanism of SARS-CoV-2 as well as potential treatment strategies for COVID-19.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-22T23:48:03.555Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/72tr8jj8y4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/72tr8jj8y4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Man Lung","last_name":"Yeung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Vasopressin","Screening","RNA Knockdown","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zkgmjzrcyk","title":"High definition analysis of host protein stability during human cytomegalovirus infection reveals antiviral factors and viral evasion mechanisms, Nightingale et al.","description":"Human cytomegalovirus (HCMV) is an important pathogen with multiple immune evasion strategies, including virally facilitated degradation of host antiviral restriction factors. Here, we describe a multiplexed approach to discover proteins with innate immune function on the basis of active degradation by the proteasome or lysosome during early phase HCMV infection. Using three orthogonal proteomic/transcriptomic screens to quantify protein degradation, with high confidence we identified 35 proteins enriched in antiviral restriction factors. A final screen employed a comprehensive panel of viral mutants to predict viral genes that target >250 human proteins. This approach revealed Helicase-like Transcription Factor (HLTF), a DNA helicase important in DNA repair, potently inhibits early viral gene expression but is rapidly degraded during infection. The functionally unknown HCMV protein UL145 facilitates HLTF degradation by recruiting the Cullin4 E3 ligase complex. Our approach and data will enable further identifications of innate pathways targeted by HCMV and other viruses.\n\nSee https://www.cell.com/cell-host-microbe/fulltext/S1931-3128(18)30381-0    for full publication.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-08-19T19:48:42.342Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zkgmjzrcyk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zkgmjzrcyk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Weekes","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Virology","Proteomics","Human Cytomegalovirus"],"journal_issn":["19313128"]},{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"v4zbbbsr5t","title":"VPS33A and VPS18 orchestrate porcine epidemic diarrhea virus replication by modulating autophagic flux","description":"Completion of autophagy through autophagosome-lysosome fusion represents a critical regulatory checkpoint that is frequently manipulated during viral infection, yet how coronaviruses exploit this terminal step remains incompletely understood. Porcine epidemic diarrhea virus (PEDV), a highly pathogenic alphacoronavirus, has been reported to induce autophagy, but whether and how complete autophagic flux contributes to viral replication is unclear. Here, using a genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screen in Vero cells, we identify the HOPS complex subunits VPS33A and VPS18 as essential host factors for PEDV infection. Genetic ablation of either VPS33A or VPS18 profoundly suppresses viral progeny production and arrests autophagic flux at the autophagosome stage, demonstrating that PEDV replication is associated with HOPS-mediated autolysosome formation. Domain-mapping analyses further reveal that the structural integrity of VPS33A and the α-solenoid plus RING domains of VPS18 are indispensable for both autophagosome-lysosome fusion and efficient viral replication. Mechanistically, we show that PEDV non-structural proteins nsp3 and nsp4 physically interact with VPS33A and VPS18. While either protein alone initiates autophagosome formation, their coordinated action contributes to drive complete autophagy in a HOPS-dependent manner. Collectively, our findings uncover a coronavirus strategy that actively promotes autophagic completion through VPS33A and VPS18, two core subunits of the HOPS complex, to support replication, thereby establishing autolysosome formation as a critical host process exploited by PEDV.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-05-18T15:12:38.75Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/v4zbbbsr5t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/v4zbbbsr5t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"liang","last_name":"guo","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"the National Key Research and Development Program of China","grant_id":"2023YFC3404301, 2023YFF1000204, and 2021YFA0805901"},{"id":null,"name":"the Hainan Provincial Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"323QN261"}],"categories":["Virus","Animal"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wph373m86f","title":"RNA-seq (FPKM), RT-qPCR, and qPCR datasets of host responses in Orientia tsutsugamushi-infected mouse macrophages","description":"This dataset contains gene expression and validation data derived from primary mouse macrophages infected with Orientia tsutsugamushi. The primary dataset consists of a genome-wide RNA-seq expression matrix reported as FPKM (Fragments Per Kilobase of transcript per Million mapped reads) values for 28,000+ genes, aligned to the Mus musculus reference genome (mm10). The matrix includes genomic annotations (gene ID, chromosome, genomic coordinates) and normalized expression values across multiple experimental conditions and biological replicates.\nIn addition to the RNA-seq dataset, the file includes qRT-PCR validation data for selected host genes (Figures 1, 4, and 5), with expression levels normalized to Gapdh. The dataset also contains bacterial burden measurements quantified by qPCR targeting the 47-kDa gene, expressed as gene copy number per sample.\nTogether, these data provide both transcriptome-wide expression profiles (FPKM) and targeted validation of host response and bacterial load under different infection conditions.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-20T15:01:42.653Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wph373m86f","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wph373m86f"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Dario","last_name":"Villacreses","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"AI193808"},{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"AI132674"},{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"AI007526"},{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"AI060549"},{"id":"https://ror.org/016tfm930","name":"The University of Texas Medical Branch at Galveston","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Immunology","RNA Sequencing","Host Defense","Innate Immunity"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":120629,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":159,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":120629,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":120076,"restricted":62,"embargoed":491},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2060},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":616},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":443},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":401},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":395},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":390},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":357},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":308},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":243},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":207}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":118042},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1900},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":178},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":120},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":75},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":54},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":36},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":28},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":491},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":400},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":350},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":319},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":289}]}}